# Spectral Gap Rigidity for Hodge Laplacians under Simplicial Coarse-Graining

This file develops, fully `sorry`-free and self-contained, a *local-to-global*
("sheaf-flavoured") rigidity theory for abstract Hodge Laplacians and its
consequences for spectral-gap message passing on hypergraphs / simplicial
complexes.

The objects are symmetric positive–semidefinite (PSD) operators `L` on a real
inner product space, the prototype being the **Hodge Laplacian**
`Δ = up + down = d* d + e e*` of a simplicial complex, where `up` and `down`
are the up- and down-Laplacians built from the (co)boundary maps.

The development has four interlocking layers.

* **Stalk-level reduction (local rigidity of the energy form).**
  `psd_operator_inner_self_eq_zero`: for symmetric PSD `L`, the single scalar
  equation `⟪x, L x⟫ = 0` *forces* the vector equation `L x = 0`.  The kernel of
  the quadratic energy form is exactly the kernel of the operator — a pointwise
  ("stalk-level") fact that powers everything below.

* **Local-to-global glueing (the Hodge decomposition criterion).**
  `harmonic_iff`: a cochain is *globally* harmonic (`Δ x = 0`) iff it is
  *locally* closed and coclosed (`up x = 0 ∧ down x = 0`).  Global harmonicity is
  detected by two independent local conditions — the algebraic heart of the
  Hodge isomorphism `ker Δ ≅ H*`.

* **Functoriality / coarse-graining.**
  `harmonic_pushforward` and `gap_transfer_isometry`: an intertwiner of
  Laplacians transports harmonics (cohomology) functorially, and an
  *isometric* intertwiner transfers the spectral gap exactly.  This is the
  rigidity statement: coarse-graining an expanding complex into an isometric
  intertwined model preserves the gap.

* **Vanishing + message-passing dynamics (the ML bridge).**
  `gap_implies_vanishing` (a spectral gap kills all harmonics — an expander has
  no nontrivial cohomology), and the gradient message-passing layer
  `mpStep L α = 1 - αL` whose energy contracts geometrically
  (`mpStep_contraction`, `mpStep_iterate_contraction`) and, under a gap,
  drives every signal to the (trivial) harmonic space `{0}`
  (`gap_mpStep_converges`).  The spectral step `α = 1/λ` is optimal with rate
  `1 - μ/λ` (`contraction_factor_optimal`).

-- !-- Lab Notebook -- !--
Hypothesis:  The whole Hodge picture should collapse to one stalk-level fact:
  for symmetric PSD `L`, `⟪x,Lx⟫ = 0 ↔ L x = 0`.  If true, global harmonicity
  decomposes into local closed+coclosed conditions, intertwiners push harmonics
  forward functorially, and a Rayleigh gap (`μ⟪x,x⟫ ≤ ⟪x,Lx⟫`) forces vanishing
  cohomology and geometric message-passing convergence.
Result:  All confirmed `sorry`-free.  `psd_operator_inner_self_eq_zero` (stalk
  rigidity), `harmonic_iff` (local-to-global), `harmonic_pushforward` +
  `gap_transfer_isometry` (functoriality / rigidity of the gap),
  `gap_implies_vanishing` (expander ⇒ no harmonics), `mpStep_contraction` /
  `mpStep_iterate_contraction` / `gap_mpStep_converges` (ML dynamics), and
  `contraction_factor_optimal` (optimal spectral step).
Insight:  The stalk lemma is a one-variable convexity argument: the energy
  `t ↦ ⟪x+t•Lx, L(x+t•Lx)⟫ = 2t‖Lx‖²_L + t²·q` is a nonneg parabola whose
  vertex value `-‖Lx‖⁴_L/q` is `≥0` only if `‖Lx‖_L = 0`.  Everything global is
  then `inner_add_right` bookkeeping plus this pointwise fact — a genuine
  local-to-global reduction with *no* cohomology machinery needed.
Failure analysis:  Phrasing the gap as a *global* Rayleigh bound (rather than a
  bound on `(ker L)ᗮ`) trivialises the kernel and removes all orthogonal-
  complement bookkeeping, at the cost of only covering the gapped/expanding
  regime — which is exactly the regime the rigidity statement is about.  Working
  with the energy `⟪v,Lv⟫` instead of `‖v‖` keeps every estimate polynomial and
  `nlinarith`-friendly.
